response:
John Snow identified the immediate source as **the public water pump on Broad Street (now Broadwick Street)**.

He concluded this from several observations in the article:
- he **talked to local residents** and, with Henry Whitehead’s help, traced the cases;
- he found that **nearly all the deaths had taken place within a short distance of the pump**;
- there were **only ten deaths in houses closer to another street pump**, and in several of those cases the families said they **still preferred Broad Street water**;
- he also noted **61 deaths among people who had been drinking from the Broad Street pump**.

The article adds an important limitation: **Snow’s chemical and microscope examination of a water sample from the pump “did not conclusively prove its danger.”**

The parish authorities responded by **removing the handle of the pump**, after Snow presented the evidence to the Board of Guardians of St James’s parish.
